return [
    'properties-of-addition-and-multiplication' => [
        'subtitle'=>'Use operation properties to rewrite expressions without changing their value.','essential_question'=>'How can properties make calculations and algebraic expressions easier to understand?','learning_targets'=>"I can identify the commutative, associative, identity, inverse, zero, and distributive properties.\nI can rewrite an expression using a named property.\nI can explain why the value stays the same.",'vocabulary'=>"Commutative property — changes the order of addends or factors\nAssociative property — changes grouping\nIdentity property — adding 0 or multiplying by 1 preserves a value\nInverse property — combines opposites or reciprocals\nZero property — any number times 0 is 0\nDistributive property — multiplies a factor across a sum or difference",'worked_examples'=>"Mental computation | 25+37+75 | Reorder and regroup as (25+75)+37. | 137.\nDistribute | 6(x+4) | Multiply 6 by both terms. | 6x+24.",'common_mistakes'=>"Using commutative property with subtraction | Subtraction is not commutative; rewrite it as adding an opposite.\nDistributing to only one term | Multiply the outside factor by every term inside the grouping symbols.",'real_life'=>'Properties support mental math, area models, budgeting, measurement, and efficient computer calculations.','videos'=>'Properties of operations | https://www.khanacademy.org/math/cc-sixth-grade-math/cc-6th-arithmetic-operations','video_chapters'=>"0:00 | Why properties work\n1:10 | Commutative\n2:20 | Associative\n3:30 | Identity and inverse\n4:40 | Distributive property",'practice_warmup'=>'Name the property: 8+13=13+8. | Commutative property of addition | The addends changed order. | Compare both sides. | Their values are equal.','guided_practice'=>"Rewrite 4(10+3). | 40+12 | Distribute 4. | Multiply both terms. | 52.\nSimplify 18+0. | 18 | Use the additive identity. | Adding zero preserves value. | 18.",'independent_practice'=>"Name the property: (2×5)×7=2×(5×7). | Associative property of multiplication | Only grouping changed. | | Both equal 70.\nRewrite 9x+9y. | 9(x+y) | Factor the common factor 9. | | Distributive property in reverse.",'challenge_practice'=>'Create two different expressions that both equal 96. Use at least two properties in each rewrite and justify every step.','master_it'=>"I can distinguish a change in order from a change in grouping.\nI can distribute and factor correctly.\nI can name the property that justifies each rewrite.",'mastery_questions'=>"Which property shows 4+9=9+4? | Associative ; Commutative ; Identity ; Zero | B\nWhich equals 5(x+3)? | 5x+3 ; 5x+15 ; x+8 ; 8x | B\nWhat is 17+0? | 0 ; 1 ; 17 ; 170 | C\nWhich property changes grouping? | Commutative ; Associative ; Inverse ; Zero | B\nFactor 8x+16. | 8(x+2) ; 8(x+16) ; x(8+16) ; 16(x+8) | A",'parent_summary'=>'Students use operation properties to make calculations efficient and justify equivalent algebraic rewrites.','parent_conversation'=>"Ask: Did the order or the grouping change?\nAsk: Which property justifies that step?\nAsk your child to show distribution with an area model.",'parent_five_minute'=>'Choose a two-digit addition or multiplication problem and find two efficient ways to calculate it using properties.','teacher_objectives'=>"Students will identify properties from symbolic examples.\nStudents will use properties to rewrite and simplify expressions.\nStudents will justify equivalence with precise vocabulary.",'teacher_pacing'=>"Launch | 5 minutes | Compare two mental strategies.\nLearn It | 15 minutes | Sort property examples.\nWatch It | 7 minutes | Pause to name each property.\nPractice It | 20 minutes | Rewrite and justify.\nMastery | 8 minutes | Include distribution and factoring.",'teacher_misconceptions'=>"Students apply commutativity to subtraction | Rewrite subtraction as addition of an opposite.\nStudents confuse order and grouping | Color-code term order and grouping symbols.\nStudents distribute incompletely | Use arrows or an area model to reach every term.",'teacher_differentiation'=>"Below Level | Use numerical examples and property cards.\nOn Level | Mix numerical and variable expressions.\nAdvanced | Justify multi-step rewrites.\nMultilingual Learners | Pair each property name with a consistent visual cue.",'teacher_formative'=>"Use a property-card sort.\nAsk students to annotate one justified rewrite.\nCollect an exit ticket with one error analysis.",'standards'=>"CCSS.MATH.CONTENT.6.EE.A.3 — Apply properties to generate equivalent expressions.\nCCSS.MATH.CONTENT.7.EE.A.1 — Add, subtract, factor, and expand expressions.\nCCSS.MATH.PRACTICE.MP3 and MP7 — Justify and use structure."
    ],
    'variables-and-expressions' => [
        'subtitle'=>'Translate between words, symbols, and real situations using variables and expressions.','essential_question'=>'How can an algebraic expression represent a quantity that can change?','learning_targets'=>"I can identify variables, constants, coefficients, and terms.\nI can translate verbal phrases into expressions.\nI can explain what each part means in context.",'vocabulary'=>"Variable — a symbol representing a number\nCoefficient — a number multiplying a variable\nConstant — a number without a variable\nTerm — a number, variable, or product separated by addition or subtraction\nExpression — a mathematical phrase without an equals sign",'worked_examples'=>"Translate words | Five more than three times n. | Three times n is 3n; then add 5. | 3n+5.\nInterpret | 12+4m | 12 is constant; 4 is coefficient; m is variable. | Two terms.",'common_mistakes'=>"Reversing subtraction phrases | 'Five less than n' means n−5.\nAdding an equals sign | An expression names a quantity; an equation states equality.",'real_life'=>'Expressions represent costs, distances, ages, scores, patterns, and changing measurements.','videos'=>'Writing algebraic expressions | https://www.khanacademy.org/math/cc-sixth-grade-math/cc-6th-arithmetic-operations/cc-6th-writing-expressions','video_chapters'=>"0:00 | Variables and quantities\n1:15 | Terms and coefficients\n2:30 | Addition phrases\n3:45 | Multiplication phrases\n5:00 | Subtraction order",'practice_warmup'=>'Write an expression for 7 more than x. | x+7 | More than signals addition. | Start with x and add 7. | x+7.','guided_practice'=>"Write twice a number p minus 4. | 2p−4 | Twice p is 2p. | Then subtract 4. | 2p−4.\nIdentify the coefficient in 9y+2. | 9 | Find the factor multiplying y. | | 9.",'independent_practice'=>"Write 12 less than 5m. | 5m−12 | Preserve subtraction order. | | 5m−12.\nHow many terms are in 4a+3b−8? | 3 | Separate at addition and subtraction. | | 4a, 3b, and −8.",'challenge_practice'=>'Create a real situation modeled by 15+3x. Define the variable, identify every term, and explain how the value changes when x increases by 1.','master_it'=>"I can distinguish an expression from an equation.\nI can preserve the order of verbal subtraction.\nI can interpret every symbol and operation in context.",'mastery_questions'=>"Coefficient in 6x+4? | x ; 4 ; 6 ; 10 | C\nFive less than n is? | 5−n ; n−5 ; 5n ; n+5 | B\nWhich is an expression? | x+7 ; x+7=12 ; x>7 ; x≤7 | A\nTerms in 3a+2b−9? | 1 ; 2 ; 3 ; 4 | C\nTwice p plus 1 is? | p+2 ; 2p+1 ; 2(p+1) ; p/2+1 | B",'parent_summary'=>'Students use variables and operations to represent changing quantities and translate accurately between words and symbols.','parent_conversation'=>"Ask: What does the variable represent?\nAsk: Does the phrase require an equals sign?\nAsk your child to explain the order in a subtraction phrase.",'parent_five_minute'=>'Invent a household cost with a fixed fee and a cost per item, then write and explain an expression for the total.','teacher_objectives'=>"Students will identify parts of an expression.\nStudents will translate verbal and contextual descriptions.\nStudents will interpret expressions using units.",'teacher_pacing'=>"Launch | 5 minutes | Describe a changing cost.\nLearn It | 15 minutes | Connect words, models, and symbols.\nWatch It | 7 minutes | Pause before translation.\nPractice It | 20 minutes | Mix phrase structures.\nMastery | 8 minutes | Include interpretation.",'teacher_misconceptions'=>"Students treat adjacent symbols as addition | Connect adjacency to multiplication.\nStudents reverse less-than phrases | Use a number-line or start-change frame.\nStudents confuse expressions and equations | Highlight the equality statement.",'teacher_differentiation'=>"Below Level | Use phrase-operation cards and concrete contexts.\nOn Level | Mix multi-term phrases.\nAdvanced | Write expressions from constraints and patterns.\nMultilingual Learners | Build a phrase-to-symbol reference chart.",'teacher_formative'=>"Ask students to label parts of an expression.\nUse matching cards for words and symbols.\nEnd with one contextual translation and explanation.",'standards'=>"CCSS.MATH.CONTENT.6.EE.A.2 — Write, read, and evaluate expressions.\nCCSS.MATH.CONTENT.6.EE.A.2.B — Identify parts of expressions.\nCCSS.MATH.PRACTICE.MP2 and MP6 — Reason and communicate precisely."
    ],
    'radicals-and-integer-exponents' => [
        'subtitle'=>'Use integer exponents and radicals to represent, compare, and simplify quantities.','essential_question'=>'How are powers and roots connected as inverse operations?','learning_targets'=>"I can evaluate powers with integer exponents.\nI can use exponent rules for products and quotients.\nI can estimate and simplify square and cube roots.",'vocabulary'=>"Base — repeated factor in a power\nExponent — number of repeated factors\nRadical — symbol indicating a root\nSquare root — number whose square equals a given number\nCube root — number whose cube equals a given number",'worked_examples'=>"Exponent product | 2³×2⁴ | Add exponents with the same base. | 2⁷=128.\nEstimate root | √50 | 49<50<64. | 7<√50<8.",'common_mistakes'=>"Multiplying exponents in a product | With the same base, add exponents.\nAssuming √(a+b)=√a+√b | Roots do not distribute over addition.",'real_life'=>'Powers and roots appear in area, volume, scientific notation, growth models, engineering, and computer storage.','videos'=>'Integer exponents and radicals | https://www.khanacademy.org/math/cc-eighth-grade-math/cc-8th-numbers-operations','video_chapters'=>"0:00 | Bases and exponents\n1:20 | Product and quotient rules\n2:45 | Zero and negative exponents\n4:10 | Square roots\n5:35 | Cube roots",'practice_warmup'=>'Evaluate 3⁴. | 81 | Multiply four factors of 3. | 3×3×3×3. | 81.','guided_practice'=>"Simplify 5²×5³. | 5⁵=3125 | Same base: add exponents. | 2+3=5. | 5⁵.\nEstimate √70. | Between 8 and 9 | Use nearby perfect squares. | 64<70<81. | 8<√70<9.",'independent_practice'=>"Evaluate 2⁻³. | 1/8 | Use a⁻ⁿ=1/aⁿ. | | 1/2³=1/8.\nSimplify ∛125. | 5 | Find a number cubed to 125. | | 5³=125.",'challenge_practice'=>'Compare 2¹⁰, 4⁵, and √1,048,576 without relying only on a calculator. Explain the structural relationships.','master_it'=>"I can distinguish a base from an exponent.\nI can choose the correct exponent rule.\nI can use perfect squares and cubes to reason about roots.",'mastery_questions'=>"3⁴ equals? | 12 ; 27 ; 64 ; 81 | D\n2³×2⁴ equals? | 2⁷ ; 4⁷ ; 2¹² ; 4¹² | A\n2⁻³ equals? | −8 ; −6 ; 1/8 ; 8 | C\n√50 lies between? | 5 and 6 ; 6 and 7 ; 7 and 8 ; 8 and 9 | C\n∛64 equals? | 4 ; 8 ; 16 ; 32 | A",'parent_summary'=>'Students connect powers and roots, apply exponent rules, and estimate irrational roots using nearby perfect powers.','parent_conversation'=>"Ask: What is the repeated factor?\nAsk: Which exponent rule applies and why?\nAsk your child to bracket a root between perfect squares.",'parent_five_minute'=>'Make cards for perfect squares from 1² through 12², shuffle them, and match each value to its square root.','teacher_objectives'=>"Students will evaluate integer powers.\nStudents will apply exponent properties accurately.\nStudents will estimate and simplify square and cube roots.",'teacher_pacing'=>"Launch | 5 minutes | Notice repeated multiplication.\nLearn It | 15 minutes | Build rules from expanded form.\nWatch It | 7 minutes | Predict each rule.\nPractice It | 20 minutes | Mix powers and roots.\nMastery | 8 minutes | Include estimation.",'teacher_misconceptions'=>"Students multiply base and exponent | Expand the power.\nStudents apply rules to unlike bases | Require a same-base check.\nStudents treat roots as division by two | Connect roots to area and inverse operations.",'teacher_differentiation'=>"Below Level | Use exponent tiles and perfect-square charts.\nOn Level | Mix integer exponents and radical estimates.\nAdvanced | Simplify radical expressions and scientific notation.\nMultilingual Learners | Pair power/root terms with diagrams.",'teacher_formative'=>"Ask for expanded form before a rule.\nUse a perfect-square number-line check.\nCollect one exponent-rule error analysis.",'standards'=>"CCSS.MATH.CONTENT.8.EE.A.1 — Know and apply properties of integer exponents.\nCCSS.MATH.CONTENT.8.NS.A.2 — Approximate irrational numbers.\nCCSS.MATH.PRACTICE.MP7 and MP8 — Use structure and repeated reasoning."
    ],
    'evaluate-expressions' => [
        'subtitle'=>'Substitute values and apply the order of operations to evaluate expressions accurately.','essential_question'=>'How can substitution and structure help us find an expression’s value?','learning_targets'=>"I can substitute a value for each variable.\nI can evaluate using grouping, exponents, and operation pairs.\nI can check whether my result is reasonable.",'vocabulary'=>"Evaluate — find a numerical value\nSubstitute — replace a variable with a value\nNumerical expression — expression containing numbers and operations\nGrouping symbols — parentheses, brackets, or fraction bars",'worked_examples'=>"Evaluate 3x+5 for x=4 | Substitute 4. | 3(4)+5. | 17.\nEvaluate 2a²−b for a=3,b=4 | Exponent first. | 2(9)−4. | 14.",'common_mistakes'=>"Failing to use parentheses for negative substitutions | Replace x with (−3) so signs remain clear.\nMultiplying before evaluating an exponent | Exponents are evaluated before multiplication.",'real_life'=>'Evaluation calculates total costs, distances, areas, scores, conversions, and predicted values from formulas.','videos'=>'Evaluating expressions | https://www.khanacademy.org/math/cc-sixth-grade-math/cc-6th-arithmetic-operations/cc-6th-evaluating-expressions','video_chapters'=>"0:00 | Meaning of evaluate\n1:15 | Substitution\n2:35 | Multiple variables\n3:55 | Negative values\n5:15 | Order of operations",'practice_warmup'=>'Evaluate 2x+1 when x=5. | 11 | Substitute 5 for x. | 2(5)+1. | 11.','guided_practice'=>"Evaluate 4m−7 for m=6. | 17 | Substitute 6. | 24−7. | 17.\nEvaluate x²+3y for x=−2,y=5. | 19 | Use parentheses around −2. | (−2)²+15. | 19.",'independent_practice'=>"Evaluate 5(a+2) for a=3. | 25 | Substitute, then group. | | 5(5)=25.\nEvaluate p/q+4 for p=18,q=3. | 10 | Substitute and divide. | | 18/3+4=10.",'challenge_practice'=>'Create an expression with two variables whose value is 24 when x=3 and y=2. Find a second pair that also produces 24 and verify both.','master_it'=>"I can substitute every variable consistently.\nI can preserve grouping around negative values.\nI can show one order-of-operations step at a time.",'mastery_questions'=>"3x+5 when x=4 equals? | 12 ; 17 ; 20 ; 32 | B\n2a² when a=3 equals? | 6 ; 12 ; 18 ; 36 | C\nWhy use parentheses around −3? | Decoration ; Preserve the sign ; Change value ; Remove exponent | B\n5(m+2) when m=4 equals? | 22 ; 26 ; 30 ; 32 | C\nIf p=18,q=3, p/q+4 equals? | 6 ; 10 ; 22 ; 58 | B",'parent_summary'=>'Students replace variables with given values and use the order of operations to calculate expressions reliably.','parent_conversation'=>"Ask: What value replaces each variable?\nAsk: Which operation comes next?\nAsk your child to estimate whether the answer is reasonable.",'parent_five_minute'=>'Write a simple expression such as 4x+3, choose three values for x, and make a small input-output table.','teacher_objectives'=>"Students will substitute into one- and multi-variable expressions.\nStudents will evaluate with correct operation order.\nStudents will communicate and check their steps.",'teacher_pacing'=>"Launch | 5 minutes | Use a formula with a familiar input.\nLearn It | 15 minutes | Model substitution notation.\nWatch It | 7 minutes | Pause before each operation.\nPractice It | 20 minutes | Include negatives and fractions.\nMastery | 8 minutes | Require visible work.",'teacher_misconceptions'=>"Students concatenate instead of multiply | Reinforce that 3x means 3 times x.\nStudents lose negative signs | Require parentheses around substituted negatives.\nStudents use left-to-right for all operations | Mark priority levels first.",'teacher_differentiation'=>"Below Level | Use color-matched variables and values.\nOn Level | Mix multi-variable expressions.\nAdvanced | Evaluate formulas with rational values.\nMultilingual Learners | Use replace-then-calculate sentence frames.",'teacher_formative'=>"Watch substitutions before calculation.\nUse one negative-value check.\nAsk students to explain which step is justified next.",'standards'=>"CCSS.MATH.CONTENT.6.EE.A.2.C — Evaluate expressions at specific values.\nCCSS.MATH.CONTENT.6.EE.A.1 — Evaluate expressions with exponents.\nCCSS.MATH.PRACTICE.MP1 and MP6 — Persevere and attend to precision."
    ],
    'equivalent-expressions' => [
        'subtitle'=>'Generate and verify expressions that have the same value for every allowed input.','essential_question'=>'How can we prove that two expressions are equivalent?','learning_targets'=>"I can combine like terms.\nI can use the distributive property to expand or factor.\nI can justify why two expressions are equivalent.",'vocabulary'=>"Equivalent expressions — expressions with the same value for all inputs\nLike terms — terms with identical variable parts\nExpand — use distribution to remove grouping\nFactor — rewrite a sum as a product\nSimplify — rewrite an expression in a more useful form",'worked_examples'=>"Combine like terms | 4x+3+2x−5 | Group 4x+2x and 3−5. | 6x−2.\nExpand | 3(2x−4) | Distribute 3 to both terms. | 6x−12.",'common_mistakes'=>"Combining unlike terms | Variable parts and exponents must match.\nChanging signs while distributing a negative | Multiply every term, including its sign.",'real_life'=>'Equivalent expressions provide different ways to represent area, cost, perimeter, patterns, and computer formulas.','videos'=>'Equivalent expressions | https://www.khanacademy.org/math/cc-sixth-grade-math/cc-6th-arithmetic-operations/cc-6th-equivalent-expressions','video_chapters'=>"0:00 | Meaning of equivalent\n1:15 | Like terms\n2:35 | Distribution\n3:55 | Factoring\n5:15 | Verifying equivalence",'practice_warmup'=>'Simplify 3x+5x. | 8x | The terms are alike. | Add coefficients. | 8x.','guided_practice'=>"Simplify 7a+4−2a+9. | 5a+13 | Group like terms. | 7a−2a and 4+9. | 5a+13.\nExpand −2(x−6). | −2x+12 | Distribute −2. | Multiply both terms. | −2x+12.",'independent_practice'=>"Factor 12x+18. | 6(2x+3) | Find the GCF. | | Distribute to verify.\nAre 4(x+2) and 4x+8 equivalent? | Yes | Distribute 4. | | Both simplify to 4x+8.",'challenge_practice'=>'Write three visibly different expressions equivalent to 6x+12. Include an expanded form, a factored form, and a form containing like terms; prove each.','master_it'=>"I can identify like terms before combining them.\nI can distribute positive and negative factors.\nI can verify equivalence algebraically or with multiple inputs.",'mastery_questions'=>"3x+5x equals? | 8 ; 8x ; 15x ; 3x² | B\n3(2x−4) equals? | 6x−4 ; 6x−12 ; 5x−7 ; 6x+12 | B\nWhich terms are like? | 3x and 3y ; 4x and −2x ; x and x² ; 5 and 5x | B\nFactor 8x+12. | 4(2x+3) ; 8(x+12) ; 2(4x+12) ; x(8+12) | A\nAre 2(x+5) and 2x+10 equivalent? | Yes ; No ; Only x=0 ; Only x=5 | A",'parent_summary'=>'Students combine like terms and use distribution or factoring to create and prove equivalent expressions.','parent_conversation'=>"Ask: Which terms are alike and why?\nAsk: Did every term receive the factor?\nAsk your child to substitute a value as a quick check.",'parent_five_minute'=>'Write an expression such as 2(x+4)+3x, simplify it together, and test both forms using two values of x.','teacher_objectives'=>"Students will combine like terms accurately.\nStudents will expand and factor expressions.\nStudents will justify equivalence using properties and substitution checks.",'teacher_pacing'=>"Launch | 5 minutes | Compare two forms of an area.\nLearn It | 15 minutes | Model like-term structure and distribution.\nWatch It | 7 minutes | Predict each rewrite.\nPractice It | 20 minutes | Mix expansion and factoring.\nMastery | 8 minutes | Require proof.",'teacher_misconceptions'=>"Students combine unlike terms | Compare complete variable parts.\nStudents add exponents when adding terms | Contrast addition with multiplication.\nStudents miss a distributed term | Use arrows or area boxes.",'teacher_differentiation'=>"Below Level | Use algebra tiles and color-coded like terms.\nOn Level | Mix rational coefficients and negatives.\nAdvanced | Compare multiple equivalent forms strategically.\nMultilingual Learners | Use same-variable-part sentence frames.",'teacher_formative'=>"Use a like-term card sort.\nAsk students to expand and then refactor one expression.\nCollect a short equivalence proof as an exit ticket.",'standards'=>"CCSS.MATH.CONTENT.6.EE.A.3–4 — Apply properties and identify equivalent expressions.\nCCSS.MATH.CONTENT.7.EE.A.1–2 — Rewrite expressions in different forms.\nCCSS.MATH.PRACTICE.MP3 and MP7 — Justify and use structure."
    ]
];
